  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The operational benefits, particularly headway improvements, are severely constrained during the transition phase when agencies must operate a mixed fleet of human-driven and autonomous vehicles.",
      "what": "This study models and simulates semi-on-demand hybrid-route transit services using Shared Autonomous Vehicles (SAVs) across ten bus routes in Munich, Germany during off-peak hours. The proposed service model requires SAVs to visit all scheduled fixed-route stops before deviating to pick up and drop off passengers in designated flexible-route zones. Using a combination of analytical modeling and agent-based simulation, the research evaluates optimal fleet sizes, vehicle capacities, and operational performance during a gradual transition from human drivers to autonomous fleets.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that SAVs can satisfy up to 80% more passenger requests and cut generalized travel costs by up to 50% compared to traditional fixed-route services, proving that autonomous operations can make hybrid-route transit economically viable."
    }
